#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183890 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "9ef9f522-9802-5d91-8610-1341722d7920" :question/math-academy-id "e-1429" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2 +7x -13$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n$-(x^2 -7x +13)$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 -7x+13\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x +{\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwriting the constant term ${\\color{purple}{13}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2} -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x- {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2 -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x- {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2 +\\dfrac{3}4\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} + 7x - 13 &= - ((x - \\frac{7}{2})^{2} + \\frac{3}{4}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{4} - (x - \\frac{7}{2})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183891 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "b38247c0-cb0a-5fa0-afed-45c7e1ba9e3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1430" :question/problem "Rewrite $2x^2 - 6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2$. So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2\\left(x^2 - 3x + \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 3x + {\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwriting the constant term\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nlast:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x^{2} - 3x + \\frac{1}{2}) &= 2((x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - \\frac{7}{4}) \\\\\n&= 2(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{7}{4} \\\\\n&= 2(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - \\frac{7}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184059 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/id #uuid "10318f8d-0396-53de-af20-e3b9ee6ac3d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9932" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2+6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n$-(x^2 - 6x -1)$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 6x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2- {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 10\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} + 6x + 1 &= - ((x - 3)^{2} - 10) \\\\\n&= - (x - 3)^{2} + 10 \\\\\n&= 10 - (x - 3)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184060 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "8b469eb3-6577-588c-aa5b-7d168ea6a0dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9933" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2x^2+20x - 40$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2$. So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2\\left(x^2 - 10x + 20\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 10x + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}2}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue} 5}^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}20}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{blue} 5}^2} - {\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 -{\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 - 5\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(x^{2} - 10x + 20) &= -2((x - 5)^{2} - 5) \\\\\n&=-2(x - 5)^{2} + (-2) \\cdot (-5) \\\\\n&=-2(x - 5)^{2} + 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "The plus sign inside the square produces linear term $-16k$ after subtraction, opposite the required $+16k$. Its constant also does not match $-8$." :answer/id #uuid "eee6e16d-c441-59c2-ada6-182ccb2357e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38 - (k + 8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180796} {:answer/feedback "The binomial is correct, but expanding gives constant $46-64=-18$. The original expanded expression has constant $-8$, so the outside constant must be $56$." :answer/id #uuid "1a06d1d8-cedb-5250-af12-c11e07ef5ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46 - (k - 8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180797} {:answer/feedback "Inside the negative factor, $k^2-16k+8=(k-8)^2-56$. Distributing the negative gives $56-(k-8)^2$." :answer/id #uuid "1cfd568b-37ce-5df5-a9fa-8b73150eeec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56 - (k - 8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180798} {:answer/feedback "This expands to $-k^2+16k-10$. The required constant is $-8$, so the outside value is $64-8=56$, not $54$." :answer/id #uuid "41108c78-a181-5696-9455-3e8d8e60d016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54 - (k - 8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180799} {:answer/feedback "Expanding $(k+8)^2$ makes the linear term $-16k$ after subtraction. The original expression expands to $-k^2+16k-8$, so the binomial must use $k-8$." :answer/id #uuid "6c0eb7e4-a69c-56a4-a6f6-2405cb481407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42 - (k + 8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "Inside the negative factor, $k^2-16k+8=(k-8)^2-56$. Distributing the negative gives $56-(k-8)^2$." :answer/id #uuid "1cfd568b-37ce-5df5-a9fa-8b73150eeec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56 - (k - 8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180798} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d5b430-77fe-5def-a568-ffee537c9d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180795}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b217693-bc6b-565e-8cf8-4ff0d3fc9863" :question/math-academy-id "q-112300" :question/problem "Rewrite $- (k^{2} - 16k + 8)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Inside the negative factor, $k^2-16k+8=(k-8)^2-56$. Distributing the negative gives $56-(k-8)^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "856e5cd2-ce0b-5e19-90c3-099ed2452957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250411} {:answer/id #uuid "72ffbf6b-9da7-54d1-9e44-d19eed586ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250412} {:answer/id #uuid "574728f4-09da-577a-88ac-453f26067779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250413} {:answer/id #uuid "25c8ab92-df54-5ad7-bb0e-109764b78a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250414} {:answer/id #uuid "70ad33b2-ee8a-5b28-98b0-40743e1628d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "574728f4-09da-577a-88ac-453f26067779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250413} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd914dfc-842b-5eab-8855-4b5cea6d36cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250410}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "326e15b0-6aef-59e0-9f84-d0b719d881bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-140318" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+4x-12)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x-12\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x-12\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $12$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-{2}^{2}-12\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-12\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-16.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+4x-12) & =-({(x+2)}^{2}-16) \\\\  & =-{(x+2)}^{2}+16 \\\\  & =16-(x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92324395-65e2-5074-9ab3-d1fed5aad71c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10-(y-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250418} {:answer/id #uuid "66263f55-7671-54c7-b9de-dda2ebe88ab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15-(y-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250419} {:answer/id #uuid "e6647176-4edb-5bce-b395-f92cac060123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250420} {:answer/id #uuid "b2150979-7908-5780-8d5c-d9bfdd7b97f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250421} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab200dd-8e7a-5b3e-88a0-e2d99dbf810f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6647176-4edb-5bce-b395-f92cac060123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250420} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f9e4be2-2c71-59ac-894a-bdb97a475a30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250417}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56ba52e8-220b-5a72-98e6-d401a8d1bea8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140321" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}+10y+20)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+10y+20\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 5y+20\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $20$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-{5}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+5)^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}+5y+20) & =-({(y+5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-{(y+5)}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =5-(y+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2083c0f8-4081-5afe-a3e5-0bfed317b325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250425} {:answer/id #uuid "a12fbb90-e231-5de9-8b01-0ea95084ad49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250426} {:answer/id #uuid "e1167a31-1c2f-5bd6-9914-5da7a1b39921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250427} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2397d3-5814-5bca-b9b8-fc9c72a2224b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{4}-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250428} {:answer/id #uuid "7166a7d7-613b-52fa-afc7-4aa72133f8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7166a7d7-613b-52fa-afc7-4aa72133f8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250429} :answer-field/id #uuid "b892fd63-07c6-5d28-a75d-acbd410c9249" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250424}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "010471e5-7a80-5814-a376-6e6144513884" :question/math-academy-id "q-140330" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+7x+10)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+7x+10) & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd7569d3-8be0-5e91-8e37-64ab1a797b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250432} {:answer/id #uuid "713ee8ca-4fc0-5a69-9822-b70bffbc5202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250433} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e8a0d8-b430-5f68-8077-a2d68a71116c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250434} {:answer/id #uuid "af6ba6e0-b92d-5ea7-a928-a0eec364aa48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250435} {:answer/id #uuid "210c13e6-3768-53ce-91f3-2d63cbbc0b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-{(x+3)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af6ba6e0-b92d-5ea7-a928-a0eec364aa48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250435} :answer-field/id #uuid "6961173d-ad84-5691-9f88-899ed925c07e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250431}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3034924-9fcd-592f-bbe8-d82455e6deab" :question/math-academy-id "q-140337" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+3x+3)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x+3\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $3$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+3x+3) & =-({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa55ed30-be75-5007-9a94-c30bd5bb3d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{4}-{(x+10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250439} {:answer/id #uuid "eec7fa9d-f47b-5b99-b266-bc3b5e64deac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{4}-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250440} {:answer/id #uuid "765b85c6-7d5e-5e04-abf5-6b7212b03aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250441} {:answer/id #uuid "403fd8f8-b1aa-5e18-b9b5-ecace715b888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250442} {:answer/id #uuid "ae740ca3-0d4f-52eb-9f5e-33cbea4dedce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32-{(x+10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "403fd8f8-b1aa-5e18-b9b5-ecace715b888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250442} :answer-field/id #uuid "e67a3f81-2262-54af-8e2b-99b7d4a4ea6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250438}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0ebb993-ccf9-5c07-9aa5-b4c17d48af4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140356" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+5x+7)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+5x+7\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+7\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $7$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+5x+7) & =-({(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "The negative sign inside the square produces linear term $+6z$ after multiplication by $-9$. The original linear term is $-6z$." :answer/id #uuid "d63b121a-2d4c-5ad7-83a9-37337db28abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9(z - \\frac{1}{3})^{2} - 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181012} {:answer/feedback "The square gives the correct quadratic and linear terms, but its constant contribution is $-1$. Adding $5$ produces constant $4$, not $9$." :answer/id #uuid "da56f077-8cf7-5157-bbf1-c4135577f469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9(z + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181013} {:answer/feedback "Factoring $-9$ gives $-9(z^2+\\frac23z-1)$. The inner expression is $(z+\\frac13)^2-\\frac{10}{9}$, yielding $-9(z+\\frac13)^2+10$." :answer/id #uuid "19b4e5a2-f198-5dd9-91ee-22690b49f09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9(z + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} + 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181014} {:answer/feedback "Although the outside constant is correct, the binomial sign is not: this expands with linear term $+6z$ instead of $-6z$." :answer/id #uuid "3d23c4e0-9841-595a-881a-a7142756481a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9(z - \\frac{1}{3})^{2} + 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181015} {:answer/feedback "The binomial is correct, but the square contributes constant $-1$. Adding $8$ leaves constant $7$, whereas the original constant is $9$." :answer/id #uuid "4bff8dd4-6a62-5533-bda1-ce3db8e0709c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9(z + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} + 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "Factoring $-9$ gives $-9(z^2+\\frac23z-1)$. The inner expression is $(z+\\frac13)^2-\\frac{10}{9}$, yielding $-9(z+\\frac13)^2+10$." :answer/id #uuid "19b4e5a2-f198-5dd9-91ee-22690b49f09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9(z + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} + 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181014} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1d311a9-62f1-5a3d-8a34-95dcd2c3b053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181011}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2495fc0b-8e76-56e1-bf72-c223c52c4fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-140378" :question/problem "Rewrite $-9z^{2} - 6z + 9$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Factoring $-9$ gives $-9(z^2+\\frac23z-1)$. The inner expression is $(z+\\frac13)^2-\\frac{10}{9}$, yielding $-9(z+\\frac13)^2+10$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3bd5b3b-06bc-551f-a1c0-f7c93726986c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250446} {:answer/id #uuid "3abf4b3e-07c9-56e6-963d-d65706ac1ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20-{(y-10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250447} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fb0fea-ccc4-560c-9e56-e17b25a805cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}+{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250448} {:answer/id #uuid "70890d95-d92c-51c4-a34f-beba51f6cfe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250449} {:answer/id #uuid "6795e805-f52f-52f9-8a77-4433c1c2094c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{4}-{(y-10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70890d95-d92c-51c4-a34f-beba51f6cfe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250449} :answer-field/id #uuid "72abfaf0-7e16-5881-80e5-4fb469833fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250445}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80cdca89-f786-5241-acc4-7042ca73b8c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-140410" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+5y-5$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-5y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-5y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+5y-5 & =-({(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffef5352-b313-5b6a-bf70-1a2b23ae85f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(2x-3)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250453} {:answer/id #uuid "21ba0ec9-a3e5-588e-8c76-3928789633f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-3)}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250454} {:answer/id #uuid "3b22330e-da24-5612-b7b7-73799cee5b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(2x-3)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250455} {:answer/id #uuid "b142892e-d7ab-5062-9c67-51508b1ea339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-3)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250456} {:answer/id #uuid "e78bbb86-5bf4-5d7b-a829-3c01aa3de57d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-3)}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e78bbb86-5bf4-5d7b-a829-3c01aa3de57d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-3)}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250457} :answer-field/id #uuid "800f8719-3ba1-5905-87f6-08d432276f23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250452}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79700f2a-8dcd-5a32-9802-3c2adc6f395a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140528" :question/problem "Rewrite $2{x}^{2}-12x+16$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-6x+8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-6x+8) & =2((x-3)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f144e0f3-7696-56e9-a1b6-3c677bb60c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250460} {:answer/id #uuid "906c4120-0217-530d-ae6b-6536ad39c76e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250461} {:answer/id #uuid "a4218eca-d68b-52cb-b20b-abc2b1073822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(5x+4)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250462} {:answer/id #uuid "707da9b4-a753-5940-b39d-e6f18010ab67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250463} {:answer/id #uuid "f2670705-3457-5a69-a45a-87afb4b1e63f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-4)}^{2}-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f144e0f3-7696-56e9-a1b6-3c677bb60c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250460} :answer-field/id #uuid "4170b0a9-742b-5509-805c-3bd8ceff96b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250459}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36b3e47b-00dc-53b2-9c7f-dfb0c26561cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-140532" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{x}^{2}-40x-60$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+8x+12)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+12\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+12\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $12$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+12\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+12\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-4\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+8x+12) & =-5((x+4)^{2}-4) \\\\  & =-5{(x+4)}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d917f23a-61fc-5659-9cef-ce65d4fcec60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250467} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc1d206-f51f-5de9-b135-2924bd105e50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250468} {:answer/id #uuid "f80e56da-0b1e-5482-81fc-a46e72cee8b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250469} {:answer/id #uuid "ad744468-7075-5b78-abcc-100ba256f09b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250470} {:answer/id #uuid "7762298c-9087-5d7a-ba6b-c663a48fb213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7762298c-9087-5d7a-ba6b-c663a48fb213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250471} :answer-field/id #uuid "39381930-8011-5c66-ae6b-4e6236c5a3ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250466}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94cfd951-eb47-55d8-8db5-3c88796721c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-140536" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{x}^{2}-6x+\\frac{1}{5}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}) & =-5({(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4d72dbf-7590-54a8-82f3-e8edf30e438b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{91}{4}-{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250474} {:answer/id #uuid "46c30875-8445-53b4-9d28-e989107f7374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{109}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250475} {:answer/id #uuid "89e1a770-51c7-568b-8423-661af46be121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{91}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250476} {:answer/id #uuid "02488be0-0a74-58ce-8b8f-b82891859305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250477} {:answer/id #uuid "68be605a-37f9-5649-8765-a3268bf684f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68be605a-37f9-5649-8765-a3268bf684f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250478} :answer-field/id #uuid "95d9fd34-8448-5e10-bfb5-2a10d1e490b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250473}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f05f8c61-ac07-5593-a81b-f631e8733df1" :question/math-academy-id "q-142071" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}-11y+30)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-11y+30\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+30\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-11y+30) & =-({(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed6b0261-a846-57d5-a14f-22443f707546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250481} {:answer/id #uuid "73cbe1d4-7fc7-5344-8de1-50283dc3c1fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed6b0261-a846-57d5-a14f-22443f707546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250481} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd642314-3834-5f39-8342-90afb652d714" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250480} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de01513c-a5e8-5710-b20c-42182d8c3622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de01513c-a5e8-5710-b20c-42182d8c3622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250484} :answer-field/id #uuid "1edc500d-6f21-5835-a765-8e3eb35e2a6e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250483}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e267adc-dfc2-5e2d-8e85-425eb3a52e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-218604" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+12x+14)$ by completing the square.\n\n$-({x}^{2}+12x+14)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-(x+$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+12x+14\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+14\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-22.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+12x+14) & =-({(x+6)}^{2}-22) \\\\  & =-(x+6)^{2}+22 \\\\  & =22-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be911586-9b14-5dd7-9ddc-c2747553d8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250487} {:answer/id #uuid "917a24ba-6396-5f04-ae86-de998a7e011c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be911586-9b14-5dd7-9ddc-c2747553d8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250487} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7553f0d-408c-568b-98bf-4d5e9c3ecf86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250486} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159132be-ec3f-5c53-856d-5712270b851f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "159132be-ec3f-5c53-856d-5712270b851f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250490} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a2d0546-f840-596f-ad7e-d819d5997df2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250489}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1a7d9fa-98c4-5e40-bc45-50c47b1e6b85" :question/math-academy-id "q-218662" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{x}^{2}+16x+1$ by completing the square.\n\n$-{x}^{2}+16x+1=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-(x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}-16x-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-16x-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 8x-1\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${8}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 8x+{8}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{8}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-8)^{2}-{8}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-8)^{2}-65.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+16x+1 & =-((x-8)^{2}-65) \\\\  & =-(x-8)^{2}+65 \\\\  & =65-(x-8)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b5d113c-dff8-5359-848c-cb9940966738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b5d113c-dff8-5359-848c-cb9940966738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250493} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae5c7583-c039-5458-ba03-b70e4b9a1e39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250492} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f8db0c4-b2df-52ed-bcbb-004be85083bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f8db0c4-b2df-52ed-bcbb-004be85083bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250495} :answer-field/id #uuid "145625b6-5da8-56d3-8322-61dd79fc624c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250494} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d75548dc-64f3-5644-85b3-cb5e1b4366dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d75548dc-64f3-5644-85b3-cb5e1b4366dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250497} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a87eb0c-e54d-5280-9178-5a5d1f34d58c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250496}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32983a3e-5ad8-5b11-aeaa-c98688c6494d" :question/math-academy-id "q-218756" :question/problem "Rewrite $6{p}^{2}-12p+7$ by completing the square.\n\n$6{p}^{2}-12p+7=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(p\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6.$ So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 1p+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{6}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 1p+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{1}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(p-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(p-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6}) & =6({(p-1)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =6{(p-1)}^{2}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =6{(p-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4e6011f-cde4-5353-9da0-341cc54d9bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4e6011f-cde4-5353-9da0-341cc54d9bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250500} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe4a4169-68ed-5c6a-940c-4132a6cbf957" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250499} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6fa2de9-c2ee-575d-a2f7-deeccb26e1bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6fa2de9-c2ee-575d-a2f7-deeccb26e1bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250502} :answer-field/id #uuid "270a4a97-0cb4-517b-9dd9-d5c509cd8c62" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250501} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2498ec73-64e6-5612-82d3-a184cc200dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2498ec73-64e6-5612-82d3-a184cc200dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250504} :answer-field/id #uuid "880f07a1-8260-5d8f-812c-bd45ba637d62" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250503}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d1b8685-4e2a-5ec9-b1ad-83d2f5c1d7b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-218759" :question/problem "Rewrite $-8{x}^{2}+32x-29$ by completing the square.\n\n$-8{x}^{2}+32x-29=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8.$ So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{29}{8}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}) & =-8({(x-2)}^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3-8{(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "badde559-c240-56b0-a32b-f8ae65ea324e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{67}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250507} {:answer/id #uuid "3057a71a-0f0d-50c4-8eda-2d5828333d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{67}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250508} {:answer/id #uuid "68b02793-d346-5e5e-8b86-6ac572988ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250509} {:answer/id #uuid "5a15da04-297f-5d69-b95e-60f92d2c176f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{89}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250510} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fb4ff9-5d57-5854-9c61-39b0c95c4607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68b02793-d346-5e5e-8b86-6ac572988ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250509} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6d19984-a6c2-5e3f-9ab1-03450a94c5ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250506}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1add826e-39e7-5ce2-8d3c-d21eca337c70" :question/math-academy-id "q-35079" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2{z}^{2}-14z+16$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({z}^{2}+7z-8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+7z-8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z-8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({z}^{2}+7z-8) & =-2({(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b3d5733-24d5-5218-879b-144005024208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250514} {:answer/id #uuid "ac41a758-6623-51bc-bbf9-32e2b7f66432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250515} {:answer/id #uuid "68ac2a51-ea40-535d-9221-fd727b855447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250516} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb58b7a-f47b-5fb3-b1dc-7f3a6b14cb32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x+1)}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250517} {:answer/id #uuid "269327ae-c104-5963-9371-dcd16bcbf691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b3d5733-24d5-5218-879b-144005024208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250514} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c5e41a-4418-5321-96d6-934194732f89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250513}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ca1d25a-a8f7-54fa-8b13-40f3f076798d" :question/math-academy-id "q-52522" :question/problem "Rewrite $2{x}^{2}-4x+3$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{3}{2}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}) & =2((x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd40f0a6-2231-5696-affa-82b330ffa7b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250521} {:answer/id #uuid "93b251de-d086-5889-85c0-78704e227dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(6x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250522} {:answer/id #uuid "ced2f86f-87d5-585f-91dd-750771cc63d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250523} {:answer/id #uuid "af69af48-f942-50db-b93c-6dd9ceb122c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250524} {:answer/id #uuid "ce2e0f20-8f9c-54b7-b7d8-8b0c5bb92c72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(6x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ced2f86f-87d5-585f-91dd-750771cc63d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250523} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0f973b0-b05e-55c0-8cf5-ad2803007485" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250520}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ef409a8-4695-5cd6-bd99-388a5ee00c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-52537" :question/problem "Rewrite $6{x}^{2}-2x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6.$ So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x+{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x) & =6({(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}) \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "The negative sign produces linear term $-6y$, whereas the original has $+6y$. The binomial must use $y+1$." :answer/id #uuid "829bb4ab-d1cb-5a64-8e93-4d59d2b34760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y - 1)^{2} + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183372} {:answer/feedback "Factoring $3$ gives $3(y^2+2y+5)$. Since $y^2+2y+5=(y+1)^2+4$, the result is $3(y+1)^2+12$." :answer/id #uuid "bb45789d-be04-56ed-8435-f113628d9cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y + 1)^{2} + 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183373} {:answer/feedback "This has linear term $-6y$ because the binomial uses $y-1$. It also expands with constant $13$, not $15$." :answer/id #uuid "c0fecf75-2928-5a27-a9d6-6cd9f30be441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y - 1)^{2} + 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183374} {:answer/feedback "Squaring $3y+1$ produces leading term $9y^2$, not $3y^2$. The leading coefficient belongs outside the completed square." :answer/id #uuid "3f3ddcd2-4d11-51f1-a06f-72abf451bbfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3y + 1)^{2} + 14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183375} {:answer/feedback "The square gives the correct quadratic and linear terms, but its constant contribution is $3$. Adding $5$ yields constant $8$, not $15$." :answer/id #uuid "4a02cb19-bab9-5624-8ca4-bda22c0d6925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y + 1)^{2} + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "Factoring $3$ gives $3(y^2+2y+5)$. Since $y^2+2y+5=(y+1)^2+4$, the result is $3(y+1)^2+12$." :answer/id #uuid "bb45789d-be04-56ed-8435-f113628d9cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y + 1)^{2} + 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183373} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd36fead-dbde-5c9f-8a2d-0eaa33c2d514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183371}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b21db9d-e784-5ce2-96a2-6fd7b3008ee3" :question/math-academy-id "q-52542" :question/problem "Rewrite $3y^{2} + 6y + 15$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Factoring $3$ gives $3(y^2+2y+5)$. Since $y^2+2y+5=(y+1)^2+4$, the result is $3(y+1)^2+12$."}]}
